WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.746
Begin by framing Course Foundations and
Prerequisites as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.746 --> 00:00:10.312
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:16.598
The course will introduce students into the
algorithmic foundations and modern methods

4
00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:20.265
used for security evaluation and tools design.

5
00:00:20.265 --> 00:00:31.630
Consider this worked case: Students must
verify they have completed CSCI-351 or the

6
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:37.749
combined 4003-420 and 4003-440 sequence before
enrolling.

7
00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:44.662
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:44.662 --> 00:00:45.156
conclusion.

9
00:00:45.156 --> 00:00:50.906
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:51.026 --> 00:00:57.108
Now define Secure Software Development
Framework Overview precisely enough that

11
00:00:57.108 --> 00:01:00.757
another learner could test the definition.

12
00:01:00.757 --> 00:01:07.351
The Secure Software Development Framework
groups practices into four practice groups.

13
00:01:07.351 --> 00:01:13.913
Consider this worked case: Prepare the
Organization establishes people, processes,

14
00:01:13.913 --> 00:01:21.131
and technology for secure development, while
Protect the Software prevents unauthorized

15
00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:23.100
access to code.

16
00:01:23.101 --> 00:01:29.907
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:32.382
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:38.866
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:45.909
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

20
00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:48.022
throughout the analysis.

21
00:01:48.146 --> 00:01:55.062
With the foundation in place, examine how
Prepare the Organization Practice operates in

22
00:01:55.062 --> 00:01:56.658
a concrete sequence.

23
00:01:56.658 --> 00:02:05.024
Prepare the Organization establishes people,
processes, and technology for secure

24
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:05.861
development.

25
00:02:05.861 --> 00:02:13.252
Consider this worked case: A team creates a
secure development policy and assigns security

26
00:02:13.252 --> 00:02:15.892
roles before writing code begins.

27
00:02:15.892 --> 00:02:23.299
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

28
00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:29.064
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

29
00:02:29.064 --> 00:02:36.083
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

30
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:38.189
throughout the analysis.

31
00:02:38.306 --> 00:02:45.317
Next, compare the choices involved in Protect
the Software Practice and make the decision

32
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:46.318
criteria explicit.

33
00:02:46.318 --> 00:02:53.381
Protect the Software prevents unauthorized
access to code and development artifacts.

34
00:02:53.381 --> 00:03:00.553
Consider this worked case: Source code
repositories use role-based access control to

35
00:03:00.553 --> 00:03:05.334
restrict who can view or modify specific
modules.

36
00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:12.539
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

37
00:03:12.539 --> 00:03:13.740
the goalposts.

38
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:20.630
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

39
00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:27.638
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

40
00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:29.740
throughout the analysis.

41
00:03:29.858 --> 00:03:36.989
Apply Produce Well-Secured Software Practice
to a worked situation instead of treating it

42
00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:39.183
as an isolated definition.

43
00:03:39.183 --> 00:03:46.108
Produce Well-Secured Software addresses
security requirements, design, verification,

44
00:03:46.108 --> 00:03:47.839
and release.

45
00:03:47.839 --> 00:03:55.270
Consider this worked case: Developers complete
a security requirements document and run

46
00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:59.604
automated verification tests before deploying
the software.

47
00:03:59.604 --> 00:04:07.148
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

48
00:04:07.148 --> 00:04:07.651
step.

49
00:04:07.651 --> 00:04:12.167
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

50
00:04:12.167 --> 00:04:19.210
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

51
00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.323
throughout the analysis.

52
00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:27.096
Examine the tradeoffs in Respond to
Vulnerabilities Practice, including what

53
00:04:27.096 --> 00:04:30.493
improves and what becomes more difficult.

54
00:04:30.493 --> 00:04:37.691
Respond to Vulnerabilities identifies,
analyzes, remediates, and learns from

55
00:04:37.691 --> 00:04:39.290
discovered weaknesses.

56
00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:46.132
Consider this worked case: A security audit
finds a buffer overflow, which is patched, and

57
00:04:46.132 --> 00:04:51.149
the team updates their threat model based on
the lesson learned.

58
00:04:51.149 --> 00:04:58.055
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

59
00:04:58.055 --> 00:05:05.696
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

60
00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:12.505
Stress-test the reasoning around Security
Reasoning Process with an edge case and

61
00:05:12.505 --> 00:05:15.853
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

62
00:05:15.853 --> 00:05:24.485
Security reasoning identifies assets, trust
boundaries, threats, controls, evidence,

63
00:05:24.485 --> 00:05:28.322
residual risk, and responsibilities.

64
00:05:28.322 --> 00:05:35.096
Consider this worked case: An analyst maps a
web application's database as an asset,

65
00:05:35.096 --> 00:05:41.869
defines the browser as a trust boundary, and
lists SQL injection as a threat.

66
00:05:41.869 --> 00:05:48.322
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

67
00:05:48.322 --> 00:05:55.353
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

68
00:05:55.466 --> 00:06:03.350
Use Cryptographic Standards and Algorithms to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

69
00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.978
causes, and corrective action.

70
00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:12.322
Encryption supports confidentiality when keys
and modes are used correctly.

71
00:06:12.322 --> 00:06:19.041
Consider this worked case: AES-256 is an
approved algorithm that provides

72
00:06:19.041 --> 00:06:25.759
confidentiality for data at rest when
implemented with proper key management.

73
00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:33.338
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

74
00:06:33.338 --> 00:06:34.962
smallest plausible cause.

75
00:06:34.962 --> 00:06:42.494
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

76
00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:49.502
Connect Modes of Operation and Key Management
to the earlier ideas and explain how the

77
00:06:49.502 --> 00:06:51.802
pieces work as one system.

78
00:06:51.802 --> 00:06:58.723
Modes of operation define how a primitive
processes data larger than one block.

79
00:06:58.723 --> 00:07:05.887
Consider this worked case: CBC mode processes
multi-block data by chaining ciphertext

80
00:07:05.887 --> 00:07:13.051
blocks, and key management ensures keys are
rotated regularly to limit exposure.

81
00:07:13.052 --> 00:07:22.453
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

82
00:07:22.453 --> 00:07:24.020
across representations.

83
00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:31.786
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

84
00:07:31.898 --> 00:07:38.682
Finish with Security Evaluation and Practical
Application as a practical checklist that can

85
00:07:38.682 --> 00:07:40.769
guide a new analysis.

86
00:07:40.769 --> 00:07:47.738
Security depends on the complete protocol and
implementation, not only an algorithm name.

87
00:07:47.738 --> 00:07:55.486
Consider this worked case: A system using a
standard encryption algorithm fails security

88
00:07:55.486 --> 00:08:00.254
evaluation because the implementation has a
side-channel vulnerability.

89
00:08:00.254 --> 00:08:07.160
implementation security, highlighting
side-channel risks and validation checkpoints.

90
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:15.299
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

91
00:08:15.299 --> 00:08:16.551
verification step.

92
00:08:16.551 --> 00:08:23.201
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

93
00:08:23.201 --> 00:08:23.676
intuition.
